# Data Transmission Diagram
<small>*Note: The following interaction diagram is transcribed from my initial paper sketches, used to help me visualize the behavior of the sliding window, out-of-order reception, and fast retransmit mechanism.*</small>
```text
 Sender                                                Receiver
   |                                                      |
   | --- FLAG_DATA (Seq = 1) ---------------------------> | (Buffer = {1}, Ack=2)
   | --- FLAG_DATA (Seq = 2) ----- [ LOST! ]              |
   | --- FLAG_DATA (Seq = 3) ---------------------------> | (Out-of-order packet. Ack=2)
   | --- FLAG_DATA (Seq = 4) ---------------------------> | (Out-of-order packet. Ack=2)
   |                                                      |
   | <--- FLAG_ACK (AckNum = 2) ------------------------- |
   | <--- FLAG_ACK (AckNum = 2) --- (1st Duplicate) ----- |
   | <--- FLAG_ACK (AckNum = 2) --- (2nd Duplicate) ----- |
   | <--- FLAG_ACK (AckNum = 2) --- (3rd Duplicate) ----- |
   |                                                      |
 (Fast Retransmit Triggered!)                             |
   | --- FLAG_DATA (Seq = 2) ---------------------------> |
   |                                                      | (Finds {3,4} in buffer)
   | <--- FLAG_ACK (AckNum = 5) ------------------------- | (Acknowledges entire batch)

```

---

# Data Transmission

The transmission mechanism relies on a dynamic sliding window that efficiently combines flow control and fast recovery techniques.

## Sliding Window & Flow Control

The sender continuously transmits packets, but the number of unacknowledged packets "in flight" is limited by:

* `global_window_size` (network limit derived from the BDP calculation);
* `peer_window` (actual free buffer space advertised by the receiver).

Whenever the transmission window becomes full, the sender efficiently blocks using condition variables (`pthread_cond_wait`) until space becomes available again.

## Out-of-Order Reception

If the receiver obtains packets ahead of the expected sequence number (e.g., packet 3 while still expecting packet 2), those packets are not discarded.

Instead, they are temporarily stored inside an `std::map out_of_order`, implementing a *Selective Repeat*-style behavior.

## Fast Retransmit

If the sender receives **three consecutive duplicate ACKs**, this indicates that the receiver successfully obtained later packets but is missing a specific packet.

The sender immediately retransmits only the missing packet at the base of the transmission window, avoiding unnecessary retransmission of the entire window and eliminating the need to wait for a timeout.

## Go-Back-N on Timeout

If severe packet loss occurs and the main retransmission timer expires, the sender falls back to a Go-Back-N strategy.

In this case, all currently unacknowledged packets within the transmission window are retransmitted as a safety mechanism.

## Zero-Window Update

If the receiving application consumes data slowly, the receiver buffer may become full.

The receiver then advertises `recv_window = 0`, causing the sender to stop transmitting.

Once the application reads data from the buffer (inside `recv_data()`), the receiver proactively sends a Window Update message, allowing the sender to immediately resume transmission without waiting for additional events.
